# DTF tier manifest: maven-age-gate  (artifact-keeper#4702)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single + a canned Maven upstream (mock-maven,
# profile upstreams.mock-maven-agegate) serving com.example.agegate:widget in
# an old version (1.0.0, deployed 2024-01-02) and a young one (2.0.0, deployed
# a day ago), Last-Modified on every file, and a GA-level maven-metadata.xml
# listing both (latest/release 2.0.0).
#
# THE FEATURE (#4702): the publish-age gate, until now npm, PyPI, Go, Cargo
# and VS Code only, covers Maven (and Gradle) Remotes and the Virtuals over
# them. A version's publish time is the upstream Last-Modified of its POM.
# Every file of a younger-than-the-gate version, and its checksum, answers
# the shared 451 age_gate_blocked refusal and records a pending review; the
# GA-level maven-metadata.xml drops the version (latest/release re-pointed)
# so resolvers never pick it. An approved review serves it.
#
# THE GATES (Remote over the mock, gate on, min_age_days 7, mode
# upstream_publish_time; a Virtual with the Remote as its only member):
#   (FIX)     maven-metadata.xml lists only 1.0.0 with latest/release 1.0.0,
#             and its .sha1 matches the served body; the young jar, POM and
#             jar.sha1 answer 451 age_gate_blocked naming the package and
#             version; the young jar is never fetched upstream; one pending
#             review for 2.0.0; through the Virtual the young jar is 451 and
#             the merged metadata lists only 1.0.0; once the review is
#             approved the young jar downloads with the mock's bytes.
#             RED on 1.11.5: the age-gate config is refused for Maven (the
#             oracle then switches the gate on in the database, so the red run
#             shows what a gated Maven Remote did): metadata lists both,
#             every young file 200, no review.
#   (CONTROL) both images: the old 1.0.0 jar downloads 200 with the mock's
#             bytes from the Remote and the Virtual; the metadata .sha1
#             matches the metadata body.
#
# #6284 (GA-level metadata while dating is in progress): two more GAs on the
# mock list 150 old versions and one young one, more than the 64 POMs one
# listing dates. Back to back, the Remote's maven-metadata.xml, its .sha1,
# its .md5 and a second download, and the same through a Virtual over a
# private gated Remote (a private member turns the 60 s merge cache off):
#   (FIX)     the .sha1 and .md5 hash the body just served, and the two
#             downloads are identical. RED on 1.12.3/main: every sidecar
#             request ran another filter pass that dated 64 more versions, so
#             it hashed a longer document and the next download listed more.
#   (CONTROL) the young 2.0.0 is never listed; a listing after the 15 s memo
#             window has dated more versions than the first (dating goes on).
#
# #6345 (review decisions and the Virtual's 60 s merge cache): after the
# approval above, the public Virtual over the public gated Remote caches a
# merge listing 1.0.0 and 2.0.0 (fully dated, so cacheable). Then, each read
# right after the decision:
#   (FIX)     rejecting the review: the Virtual's metadata withholds 2.0.0
#             (latest/release 1.0.0) at once; approving then reopening it:
#             the approval lists 2.0.0 at once and the reopen withholds it at
#             once. RED on main: the Virtual keeps serving the cached merge
#             (2.0.0 listed after the rejection and the reopen) for up to 60 s.
#   (CONTROL) after the rejection the Remote's own metadata withholds 2.0.0,
#             the Virtual's .sha1 matches the body it served, and the young
#             jar answers 451 through the Virtual.
#
# #6382 (fixed by artifact-keeper#6411): GA-level metadata the gate cannot
# filter. On the mock, com.example.agegate:broken lists 1.0.0 and the young
# 2.0.0 without the groupId/artifactId the backend's parser needs, and :latin
# is a well-formed ISO-8859-1 document with a non-UTF-8 byte:
#   (FIX)     the gated Remote answers both documents and their .sha1 with
#             the gate's retryable 503 age_gate_unavailable naming the
#             package, and the gated Virtual does the same for broken and its
#             .sha1. RED on main: the Remote serves both unfiltered upstream
#             lists (2.0.0 included) and their .sha1 with 200, so a lenient
#             resolver picks the young version whose download then fails;
#             the Virtual drops the member document it cannot parse and
#             answers 404, which Maven caches as "no such artifact", instead
#             of the retryable 503.
#   (GUARD)   #6411: the group-level plugin-prefix document
#             org/apache/maven/plugins/maven-metadata.xml (no version index)
#             is still 200 byte for byte through the gated Remote with a
#             matching .sha1, and 200 with both prefixes through the Virtual.
#   (CONTROL) the parseable widget metadata stays 200 on both.
#
# The age gate has no record-only mode (that is the scan policy's
# `record_only`); the sanctioned way to let a young version through is an
# approved review, which this tier exercises instead.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: admin login + setup + a few dozen GETs back to back.
COVERS="6284 6345 6382"
PROFILES="storage.filesystem upstreams.mock-maven-agegate"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
